1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,079
My father.

2
00:00:02,459 --> 00:00:04,788
Behold, the enemy's ships came.

3
00:00:05,168 --> 00:00:09,543
My cities were burned, and they
did evil things in my country.

4
00:00:09,923 --> 00:00:15,193
Does my father not know that all my troops
and chariots are in the land of Hatti,

5
00:00:15,193 --> 00:00:17,926
and all my ships are in the land of Lukka?

6
00:00:18,306 --> 00:00:21,360
Thus the country is abandoned to itself.

7
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:27,405
Let my father know it: the seven ships of
the enemy that came here have done us much

8
00:00:27,405 --> 00:00:27,877
damage.

9
00:00:28,777 --> 00:00:34,115
Those words were pressed into wet clay
more than three thousand years ago by

10
00:00:34,115 --> 00:00:40,014
Ammurapi, the last king of Ugarit, a wealthy
port on the coast of what is now Syria.

11
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:43,092
We do not know if help ever came.

12
00:00:43,472 --> 00:00:49,170
We do know that, within a few years at most,
Ugarit was burned and abandoned, and no

13
00:00:49,170 --> 00:00:51,816
one ever lived in the great city again.

14
00:00:52,716 --> 00:00:57,967
You've probably never heard
of the year 1177 B.C.E.

15
00:00:58,347 --> 00:01:03,098
But in the span of roughly fifty years
around that date, the Hittite Empire

16
00:01:03,098 --> 00:01:03,668
vanished.

17
00:01:04,048 --> 00:01:07,155
The palaces of Mycenaean Greece burned.

18
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:09,694
Ugarit was wiped from the map.

19
00:01:10,074 --> 00:01:12,503
Egypt survived, but was broken.

20
00:01:12,883 --> 00:01:17,688
And in Greece, the art of writing
disappeared for almost four hundred years.

21
00:01:18,588 --> 00:01:24,175
For a long time, the blame fell on a
mysterious band of raiders that the Egyptians

22
00:01:24,175 --> 00:01:28,877
carved into their temple walls, a
people we now call the Sea Peoples.

23
00:01:29,257 --> 00:01:34,967
But the evidence that has come out of the
ground, out of ancient lake beds, out of

24
00:01:34,967 --> 00:01:40,677
tree rings and out of royal letters over
the last few decades tells a far stranger

25
00:01:40,677 --> 00:01:42,766
and far more unsettling story.

26
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:49,527
This is the story of the end of the Bronze
Age, of the first truly connected world,

27
00:01:49,527 --> 00:01:51,292
and of how it fell apart.

28
00:01:51,672 --> 00:01:53,544
So, what really killed them?

29
00:01:53,924 --> 00:01:55,179
Let's find out.

30
00:02:11,678 --> 00:02:18,163
In 1887, a local woman digging for fertile
soil in the ruins at Amarna, in Middle

31
00:02:18,163 --> 00:02:22,407
Egypt, is said to have found
a cache of clay tablets.

32
00:02:22,787 --> 00:02:28,344
They were covered not in hieroglyphs, but
in cuneiform, the wedge-shaped script of

33
00:02:28,344 --> 00:02:29,157
Mesopotamia.

34
00:02:29,537 --> 00:02:35,102
Many were broken or lost as they passed
through the hands of dealers, but in the end

35
00:02:35,102 --> 00:02:38,348
around three hundred and
eighty of them survived.

36
00:02:38,728 --> 00:02:41,586
Today we call them the Amarna Letters.

37
00:02:42,486 --> 00:02:48,599
They turned out to be the diplomatic archive
of the Egyptian court in the fourteenth

38
00:02:48,599 --> 00:02:54,349
century B.C.E., during the reigns of
Amenhotep the Third and his son Akhenaten.

39
00:02:54,729 --> 00:02:57,339
And what they reveal is astonishing.

40
00:02:57,719 --> 00:03:02,948
They show a world of great kings who wrote
to each other constantly, in a shared

41
00:03:02,948 --> 00:03:07,784
diplomatic language, Akkadian, and who
called each other by a single word.

42
00:03:08,164 --> 00:03:08,912
Brother.

43
00:03:09,812 --> 00:03:15,207
There was the king of Egypt, the richest
of them all, whose land was famous for its

44
00:03:15,207 --> 00:03:15,532
gold.

45
00:03:15,912 --> 00:03:20,557
There was the king of Babylon, ruled
at the time by a Kassite dynasty.

46
00:03:20,937 --> 00:03:26,706
There was the king of Mitanni, a powerful
state in northern Mesopotamia and Syria.

47
00:03:27,086 --> 00:03:30,856
Later there would be the
king of Assyria, rising fast.

48
00:03:31,236 --> 00:03:36,568
And to the north, in the highlands of
Anatolia, there was the king of Hatti, ruler

49
00:03:36,568 --> 00:03:37,608
of the Hittites.

50
00:03:38,508 --> 00:03:43,805
These rulers formed something that
historians have compared to a club of great

51
00:03:43,805 --> 00:03:44,280
powers.

52
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:47,572
They exchanged ambassadors and gifts.

53
00:03:47,952 --> 00:03:50,263
They married each other's daughters.

54
00:03:50,643 --> 00:03:56,010
They argued, they sulked, and they complained,
constantly, about not being treated

55
00:03:56,010 --> 00:03:57,319
with enough respect.

56
00:03:58,219 --> 00:04:03,525
One Babylonian king, Burna-Buriash, grumbled
to Pharaoh that the gold he had been

57
00:04:03,525 --> 00:04:08,765
sent, when it was put in the furnace,
turned out to be much less than its stated

58
00:04:08,765 --> 00:04:09,224
weight.

59
00:04:09,604 --> 00:04:15,241
Tushratta, king of Mitanni, pleaded again
and again for statues of solid gold that

60
00:04:15,241 --> 00:04:20,740
he believed had been promised to him, and
he was bitterly disappointed when they

61
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:23,009
arrived plated in gold over wood.

62
00:04:23,389 --> 00:04:26,643
These were not the letters
of distant strangers.

63
00:04:27,023 --> 00:04:31,722
They were the letters of a family, with
all the jealousies that come with one.

64
00:04:32,622 --> 00:04:36,738
Beyond this inner circle lay the
wider world they traded with.

65
00:04:37,118 --> 00:04:42,410
On Crete, the palace culture of the Minoans
had flourished for centuries, though by

66
00:04:42,410 --> 00:04:46,426
this time it had come under the
control of the mainland Greeks.

67
00:04:46,806 --> 00:04:53,343
On mainland Greece itself, warrior kings
ruled from fortified palaces at places like

68
00:04:53,343 --> 00:04:55,989
Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos and Thebes.

69
00:04:56,369 --> 00:04:59,152
Today we call them the Mycenaeans.

70
00:04:59,532 --> 00:05:04,838
The Hittites seem to have called them the
Ahhiyawa, and the Egyptians may have known

71
00:05:04,838 --> 00:05:06,165
their land as Tanaja.

72
00:05:07,065 --> 00:05:12,822
On the island of Cyprus, known in the texts
as Alashiya, lay the great copper mines

73
00:05:12,822 --> 00:05:14,278
of the Mediterranean.

74
00:05:14,658 --> 00:05:20,374
And along the coast of the Levant were
the trading cities of Canaan, like Byblos,

75
00:05:20,374 --> 00:05:26,161
Sidon, Tyre and Ugarit, which acted as the
great middlemen between the sea and the

76
00:05:26,161 --> 00:05:27,855
empires of the interior.

77
00:05:28,755 --> 00:05:34,177
In tomb paintings in Thebes, Egyptian
officials proudly showed foreign envoys

78
00:05:34,177 --> 00:05:36,008
arriving with their goods.

79
00:05:36,388 --> 00:05:41,264
Men from Keftiu, probably Crete,
carrying elaborate metal vessels.

80
00:05:41,644 --> 00:05:44,439
Syrians bringing horses and chariots.

81
00:05:44,819 --> 00:05:48,514
Nubians bringing gold,
ebony and animal skins.

82
00:05:48,894 --> 00:05:52,297
The world, as the Egyptians
saw it, came to them.

83
00:05:53,197 --> 00:05:55,518
Why did all of this matter so much?

84
00:05:55,898 --> 00:05:58,174
Because of a single material.

85
00:05:58,554 --> 00:05:59,391
Bronze.

86
00:06:00,291 --> 00:06:05,449
Bronze is an alloy, made mostly of
copper with around ten percent tin.

87
00:06:05,829 --> 00:06:11,376
It is harder than copper alone, and it
was the metal of weapons, armor, tools and

88
00:06:11,376 --> 00:06:13,705
prestige across the entire region.

89
00:06:14,085 --> 00:06:15,864
But here was the problem.

90
00:06:16,244 --> 00:06:20,802
Copper could be found in a number
of places, most famously Cyprus.

91
00:06:21,182 --> 00:06:22,940
Tin was much rarer.

92
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:29,032
Some of the tin used in the eastern
Mediterranean seems to have come from as far

93
00:06:29,032 --> 00:06:34,816
away as Central Asia, carried overland
through Mesopotamia, or from other distant

94
00:06:34,816 --> 00:06:38,100
sources whose exact
location is still debated.

95
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,472
That means that every bronze sword in
Mycenae, every chariot fitting in Hattusa,

96
00:06:44,472 --> 00:06:49,876
every tool in Egypt depended on a chain
of trade that crossed seas, deserts and

97
00:06:49,876 --> 00:06:54,390
mountains, and passed through the
hands of many different peoples.

98
00:06:54,770 --> 00:06:57,924
No single kingdom could make bronze alone.

99
00:06:58,304 --> 00:07:00,022
They needed each other.

100
00:07:00,922 --> 00:07:06,954
This was, in a very real sense, the first
globalized economy in the Mediterranean

101
00:07:06,954 --> 00:07:07,401
world.

102
00:07:07,781 --> 00:07:13,333
Long before Rome, long before classical
Athens, there was a network of kingdoms

103
00:07:13,333 --> 00:07:16,707
bound together by trade,
diplomacy and marriage.

104
00:07:17,088 --> 00:07:22,855
And like every globalized economy, its
great strength was also its great weakness.

105
00:07:23,755 --> 00:07:29,055
To understand just how connected this world
was, we need to go to the bottom of the

106
00:07:29,055 --> 00:07:33,973
sea, off the southern coast of Turkey,
where a ship went down more than three

107
00:07:33,973 --> 00:07:36,080
thousand three hundred years ago.

108
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:50,272
In 1982, a young sponge diver named Mehmet
Çakır was working off a rocky headland

109
00:07:50,272 --> 00:07:54,982
called Uluburun, near the town
of Kaş in southern Turkey.

110
00:07:55,363 --> 00:08:00,282
At a depth of about fifty meters, he
saw something strange on the seabed.

111
00:08:00,662 --> 00:08:04,009
He described them as
metal biscuits with ears.

112
00:08:04,909 --> 00:08:10,038
Archaeologists who heard his description
realized exactly what he had seen.

113
00:08:10,418 --> 00:08:16,030
They were oxhide ingots, slabs of copper
cast in a distinctive shape, with four

114
00:08:16,030 --> 00:08:21,713
handle-like corners, that was used to
transport metal across the Late Bronze Age

115
00:08:21,713 --> 00:08:22,139
world.

116
00:08:23,039 --> 00:08:29,164
Over the following decade, between 1984
and 1994, a team from the Institute of

117
00:08:29,164 --> 00:08:35,524
Nautical Archaeology, led first by George
Bass and then by Cemal Pulak, made more

118
00:08:35,524 --> 00:08:38,665
than twenty thousand dives to the wreck.

119
00:08:39,045 --> 00:08:44,530
What they brought up was one of the most
important archaeological discoveries of the

120
00:08:44,530 --> 00:08:45,705
twentieth century.

121
00:08:46,605 --> 00:08:50,783
The ship was small, perhaps fifteen
meters long, built of cedar.

122
00:08:51,163 --> 00:08:54,718
It sank around 1300 B.C.E.

123
00:08:55,098 --> 00:09:00,322
And its cargo was a snapshot of
the entire Late Bronze Age economy.

124
00:09:01,222 --> 00:09:06,828
There were around ten tons of copper, mostly
in the form of more than three hundred

125
00:09:06,828 --> 00:09:08,449
and fifty oxhide ingots.

126
00:09:08,829 --> 00:09:13,504
Chemical analysis suggests most
of this copper came from Cyprus.

127
00:09:13,884 --> 00:09:16,329
There was roughly a ton of tin.

128
00:09:16,709 --> 00:09:17,990
Ten to one.

129
00:09:18,370 --> 00:09:21,498
Exactly the right ratio to make bronze.

130
00:09:21,878 --> 00:09:26,551
This ship was carrying enough raw
material to equip a small army.

131
00:09:27,451 --> 00:09:29,752
But that was only the beginning.

132
00:09:30,132 --> 00:09:35,919
There were ingots of glass, in cobalt blue
and turquoise, some of them likely made

133
00:09:35,919 --> 00:09:37,613
in Egypt or Mesopotamia.

134
00:09:37,993 --> 00:09:43,627
There were large Canaanite storage jars,
many filled with terebinth resin, a

135
00:09:43,627 --> 00:09:47,037
substance probably used
in incense or perfume.

136
00:09:47,417 --> 00:09:50,102
There were logs of African ebony.

137
00:09:50,482 --> 00:09:56,106
There was elephant ivory and hippopotamus
teeth, ostrich eggshells, and spices.

138
00:09:57,006 --> 00:10:02,463
There was Mycenaean pottery from Greece,
Cypriot pottery, and Canaanite jewelry.

139
00:10:02,843 --> 00:10:08,644
There were weapons and tools whose styles
point to Italy, the Balkans, the Aegean,

140
00:10:08,644 --> 00:10:10,130
Egypt and the Levant.

141
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:16,567
There was a scarab made of gold, bearing
the name of Nefertiti, the queen of Egypt

142
00:10:16,567 --> 00:10:18,192
and wife of Akhenaten.

143
00:10:19,092 --> 00:10:22,521
And there was something
small and easily overlooked.

144
00:10:22,901 --> 00:10:28,926
A wooden writing board, made of two leaves
joined by an ivory hinge, which once held

145
00:10:28,926 --> 00:10:30,002
a layer of wax.

146
00:10:30,382 --> 00:10:33,536
Someone on board had been keeping records.

147
00:10:33,916 --> 00:10:36,249
We will never know what they wrote.

148
00:10:37,149 --> 00:10:42,093
We don't know for certain where the
Uluburun ship was going, or who owned it.

149
00:10:42,473 --> 00:10:48,345
One popular theory is that it was sailing
west from the Levant or Cyprus toward the

150
00:10:48,345 --> 00:10:53,651
Aegean, perhaps carrying a royal
shipment, a gift from one king to another.

151
00:10:54,031 --> 00:10:59,448
What is clear is that a single ship could
carry materials from at least seven or

152
00:10:59,448 --> 00:11:05,001
eight different cultures, from Africa to
the Baltic, since even amber was found on

153
00:11:05,001 --> 00:11:05,407
board.

154
00:11:06,307 --> 00:11:09,728
This was not a world of isolated kingdoms.

155
00:11:10,107 --> 00:11:16,066
It was a world where a palace in Greece
relied on copper from Cyprus, tin from the

156
00:11:16,066 --> 00:11:19,627
far east, glass from Egypt
and ivory from Africa.

157
00:11:20,007 --> 00:11:25,219
A world where any disruption in one place
could ripple outward to all the others.

158
00:11:26,119 --> 00:11:31,159
And yet, in this same century, the great
powers were also preparing for war.

159
00:11:31,539 --> 00:11:36,952
The two greatest of them, Egypt and the
Hittites, were about to meet in one of the

160
00:11:36,952 --> 00:11:39,328
largest chariot battles ever fought.

161
00:11:46,828 --> 00:11:53,574
In the spring of 1274 B.C.E., the young
pharaoh Ramesses the Second marched north

162
00:11:53,574 --> 00:11:56,906
out of Egypt at the head of a vast army.

163
00:11:57,286 --> 00:11:59,605
He was in the fifth year of his reign.

164
00:11:59,985 --> 00:12:05,685
His target was the city of Kadesh, on
the Orontes River in what is now western

165
00:12:05,685 --> 00:12:11,311
Syria, a strategic stronghold that Egypt
and the Hittites had fought over for

166
00:12:11,311 --> 00:12:12,188
generations.

167
00:12:13,088 --> 00:12:18,378
Waiting for him was the Hittite king,
Muwatalli the Second, with an army that,

168
00:12:18,378 --> 00:12:24,074
according to the Egyptian accounts, included
thousands of chariots gathered from all

169
00:12:24,074 --> 00:12:26,787
across the Hittite world and its allies.

170
00:12:27,687 --> 00:12:33,008
What happened next is one of the best
documented battles of the ancient world,

171
00:12:33,008 --> 00:12:35,668
because Ramesses made sure it would be.

172
00:12:36,048 --> 00:12:42,015
He had the story carved on the walls of
temples across Egypt, at Abu Simbel, at

173
00:12:42,015 --> 00:12:47,303
Karnak, at Luxor, at Abydos and at
his memorial temple, the Ramesseum.

174
00:12:48,203 --> 00:12:53,641
According to his version, two Bedouin men
approached the Egyptian camp and claimed

175
00:12:53,641 --> 00:12:56,891
that the Hittite army was
far away, to the north.

176
00:12:57,271 --> 00:13:02,071
Ramesses believed them, and pushed
ahead with only part of his forces.

177
00:13:02,451 --> 00:13:04,755
But the men were Hittite spies.

178
00:13:05,135 --> 00:13:09,658
The Hittite army was hidden just
behind the old city of Kadesh.

179
00:13:10,038 --> 00:13:15,239
Their chariots smashed into one of the
Egyptian divisions as it marched, scattered

180
00:13:15,239 --> 00:13:18,093
it, and then crashed
into the pharaoh's camp.

181
00:13:18,993 --> 00:13:24,679
In the Egyptian telling, Ramesses was
abandoned by almost everyone, and fought on

182
00:13:24,679 --> 00:13:30,506
alone, calling on the god Amun, charging
the enemy again and again until the battle

183
00:13:30,506 --> 00:13:30,997
turned.

184
00:13:31,377 --> 00:13:36,271
Reinforcements arrived just in time,
and the Hittite attack collapsed.

185
00:13:37,171 --> 00:13:39,289
It is a magnificent story.

186
00:13:39,669 --> 00:13:42,986
It is also, almost certainly, propaganda.

187
00:13:43,366 --> 00:13:47,012
When we look at what happened
afterward, the picture changes.

188
00:13:47,392 --> 00:13:49,908
Ramesses did not take Kadesh.

189
00:13:50,288 --> 00:13:52,149
He returned to Egypt.

190
00:13:52,529 --> 00:13:57,778
And the Hittites pushed further south,
taking territory that had belonged to Egypt's

191
00:13:57,778 --> 00:13:59,028
sphere of influence.

192
00:13:59,408 --> 00:14:02,340
At best, the battle was a bloody draw.

193
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:07,394
Hittite sources, for their part, seem
to have regarded it as a victory.

194
00:14:08,294 --> 00:14:12,262
But the most remarkable thing
about Kadesh is what came after.

195
00:14:12,642 --> 00:14:18,751
About fifteen years later, around 1259
B.C.E., Ramesses and a new Hittite king,

196
00:14:18,751 --> 00:14:24,705
Hattusili the Third, did something that
had never been done in quite this way

197
00:14:24,705 --> 00:14:25,246
before.

198
00:14:25,626 --> 00:14:28,482
They signed a formal,
written peace treaty.

199
00:14:29,382 --> 00:14:31,241
We have both versions.

200
00:14:31,621 --> 00:14:37,227
The Egyptian copy was carved in
hieroglyphs on the walls of Karnak and the

201
00:14:37,227 --> 00:14:37,985
Ramesseum.

202
00:14:38,365 --> 00:14:44,357
The Hittite copy was written in Akkadian
on a clay tablet, found in the ruins of the

203
00:14:44,357 --> 00:14:46,140
Hittite capital, Hattusa.

204
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:52,133
In it, the two kings swore eternal peace
and brotherhood, promised to come to each

205
00:14:52,133 --> 00:14:57,882
other's aid against enemies, and agreed to
return political refugees to one another.

206
00:14:58,782 --> 00:15:03,162
It is often called the oldest
surviving peace treaty in the world.

207
00:15:03,542 --> 00:15:09,727
A replica of the Hittite tablet now hangs
in the United Nations headquarters in New

208
00:15:09,727 --> 00:15:10,100
York.

209
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:12,282
The peace held.

210
00:15:12,662 --> 00:15:17,865
Ramesses later married a Hittite
princess, who took the Egyptian name

211
00:15:17,865 --> 00:15:19,072
Maathorneferure.

212
00:15:19,452 --> 00:15:23,054
Letters flowed back and forth
between the royal families.

213
00:15:23,434 --> 00:15:29,162
Hittite queen Puduhepa wrote directly
to Ramesses, and Ramesses wrote back.

214
00:15:30,062 --> 00:15:34,670
Ramesses the Second would go
on to rule for sixty-six years.

215
00:15:35,050 --> 00:15:37,827
He outlived many of his own children.

216
00:15:38,207 --> 00:15:44,902
By the time he died, around 1213 B.C.E.,
he had become the very image of an eternal

217
00:15:44,902 --> 00:15:45,547
pharaoh.

218
00:15:45,927 --> 00:15:51,194
To most people in Egypt, it must have
seemed that the world he ruled would last

219
00:15:51,194 --> 00:15:51,727
forever.

220
00:15:52,627 --> 00:15:53,688
It would not.

221
00:15:54,068 --> 00:15:59,232
Within a single lifetime of his death,
the whole system that he and his Hittite

222
00:15:59,232 --> 00:16:01,651
brothers had built would be in ruins.

223
00:16:02,031 --> 00:16:05,679
And the first warning signs
had already begun to appear.

224
00:16:13,179 --> 00:16:18,785
When Ramesses the Second died, he was
succeeded by his thirteenth son, Merneptah,

225
00:16:18,785 --> 00:16:22,107
already an old man by the
standards of his time.

226
00:16:22,487 --> 00:16:27,547
And within five years of taking the
throne, Merneptah faced an invasion.

227
00:16:28,447 --> 00:16:35,365
Around 1208 B.C.E., a Libyan chief named
Meryey led a great force east toward the

228
00:16:35,365 --> 00:16:38,013
fertile land of the Nile Delta.

229
00:16:38,393 --> 00:16:43,650
According to Merneptah's inscription at
Karnak, they came with their wives, their

230
00:16:43,650 --> 00:16:45,337
children and their cattle.

231
00:16:45,717 --> 00:16:47,783
They were not simply raiders.

232
00:16:48,163 --> 00:16:50,342
They were looking for a place to live.

233
00:16:51,242 --> 00:16:53,130
And they did not come alone.

234
00:16:53,510 --> 00:16:59,094
Among their allies, the Egyptian scribes
listed five peoples who came, in the words

235
00:16:59,094 --> 00:17:02,188
of the inscription, from
the lands of the sea.

236
00:17:02,568 --> 00:17:03,713
The Ekwesh.

237
00:17:04,093 --> 00:17:05,095
The Teresh.

238
00:17:05,475 --> 00:17:06,374
The Lukka.

239
00:17:06,754 --> 00:17:07,796
The Sherden.

240
00:17:08,176 --> 00:17:09,489
And the Shekelesh.

241
00:17:10,389 --> 00:17:15,913
This is one of the first times we meet
the people that modern historians call the

242
00:17:15,913 --> 00:17:16,731
Sea Peoples.

243
00:17:17,111 --> 00:17:20,407
And it is important to
notice something right away.

244
00:17:20,787 --> 00:17:23,165
Some of these names were not new.

245
00:17:23,545 --> 00:17:29,491
The Lukka were known from Hittite texts as
the people of a rugged coastal region in

246
00:17:29,491 --> 00:17:33,216
southwestern Anatolia, a
land of pirates and rebels.

247
00:17:33,596 --> 00:17:38,816
The Sherden had already appeared
generations earlier, both as raiders and as

248
00:17:38,816 --> 00:17:42,594
mercenaries, some of whom
fought in Pharaoh's own army.

249
00:17:43,494 --> 00:17:44,796
Merneptah won.

250
00:17:45,176 --> 00:17:50,947
His inscriptions boast of thousands of
enemy dead, and of the hands and other body

251
00:17:50,947 --> 00:17:54,606
parts cut from the slain
and counted by his scribes.

252
00:17:54,986 --> 00:18:00,878
He celebrated his victory with a stone
monument now known as the Merneptah Stele,

253
00:18:00,878 --> 00:18:06,697
which includes, almost in passing, the
earliest known mention of a people called

254
00:18:06,697 --> 00:18:07,206
Israel.

255
00:18:08,106 --> 00:18:13,484
But there is another line in Merneptah's
inscriptions that is easy to miss, and it

256
00:18:13,484 --> 00:18:16,501
may be one of the most
important clues of all.

257
00:18:16,881 --> 00:18:22,377
The pharaoh says that he sent grain in
ships, to keep the land of Hatti alive.

258
00:18:23,277 --> 00:18:25,404
Think about that for a moment.

259
00:18:25,784 --> 00:18:30,048
The Hittite Empire was one of
the two superpowers of its age.

260
00:18:30,428 --> 00:18:36,086
And yet here it was, apparently dependent
on grain shipments from Egypt to feed its

261
00:18:36,086 --> 00:18:36,563
people.

262
00:18:37,463 --> 00:18:41,018
The Hittite records themselves
confirm the picture.

263
00:18:41,398 --> 00:18:46,543
Letters from the late thirteenth century
speak again and again about grain.

264
00:18:46,923 --> 00:18:52,486
One letter, sent from the Hittite court
to the king of Ugarit, demands that a ship

265
00:18:52,486 --> 00:18:58,117
and crew be provided to carry two thousand
measures of grain to the Hittite port of

266
00:18:58,117 --> 00:18:58,388
Ura.

267
00:18:58,768 --> 00:19:02,929
It ends with a phrase that has
echoed through modern scholarship.

268
00:19:03,309 --> 00:19:05,817
It is a matter of life or death.

269
00:19:06,717 --> 00:19:11,901
Another letter, found at Ugarit, appears
to scold its king for not having sent

270
00:19:11,901 --> 00:19:17,484
supplies quickly enough, and asks, in effect,
whether he does not know that there is

271
00:19:17,484 --> 00:19:18,747
famine in the land.

272
00:19:19,127 --> 00:19:24,522
Something was going badly wrong in the
agricultural heartlands of Anatolia.

273
00:19:25,422 --> 00:19:28,799
At the same time, the
political order was shifting.

274
00:19:29,179 --> 00:19:34,918
In the east, the Assyrian king Tukulti-Ninurta
the First defeated a Hittite army,

275
00:19:34,918 --> 00:19:40,302
and later sacked Babylon, carrying away
the statue of its chief god, Marduk.

276
00:19:40,682 --> 00:19:45,108
The old balance of power between
the great kings was breaking apart.

277
00:19:46,008 --> 00:19:51,457
In the Hittite capital, the last great
kings, Tudhaliya the Fourth and then

278
00:19:51,457 --> 00:19:57,341
Suppiluliuma the Second, faced threats on
every side: rival branches of their own

279
00:19:57,341 --> 00:20:03,226
royal family, rebellious vassals in western
Anatolia, and new enemies on the sea.

280
00:20:04,126 --> 00:20:10,256
Suppiluliuma the Second left an inscription
that records something quite unusual for

281
00:20:10,256 --> 00:20:13,029
a land-based empire like the Hittites.

282
00:20:13,409 --> 00:20:14,687
A naval battle.

283
00:20:15,067 --> 00:20:20,456
He claims to have fought the ships of
Alashiya, of Cyprus, three times on the sea,

284
00:20:20,456 --> 00:20:22,230
and to have destroyed them.

285
00:20:22,610 --> 00:20:28,047
Who exactly was crewing those ships, and
whether Cyprus itself was the enemy or had

286
00:20:28,047 --> 00:20:30,799
been taken over by others, we do not know.

287
00:20:31,699 --> 00:20:35,299
What we can see is a
world under rising stress.

288
00:20:35,679 --> 00:20:37,637
Hunger in Anatolia.

289
00:20:38,017 --> 00:20:42,877
Peoples on the move along the coasts
of North Africa and the Aegean.

290
00:20:43,257 --> 00:20:45,767
Rivalries among the great kings.

291
00:20:46,147 --> 00:20:50,402
A sea that was becoming more
dangerous with every passing year.

292
00:20:51,302 --> 00:20:56,571
And at the center of all this, on the Syrian
coast, sat the richest trading city of

293
00:20:56,571 --> 00:20:57,142
them all.

294
00:20:57,522 --> 00:21:03,842
A city that had no idea it was about to
become the most famous casualty of the age.

295
00:21:11,342 --> 00:21:17,909
In 1928, a farmer ploughing his field near
the Syrian coast, at a place called Minet

296
00:21:17,909 --> 00:21:20,255
el-Beida, struck a stone slab.

297
00:21:20,635 --> 00:21:23,017
Beneath it was an ancient tomb.

298
00:21:23,397 --> 00:21:28,893
The next year, French archaeologists led
by Claude Schaeffer began digging at the

299
00:21:28,893 --> 00:21:31,471
large mound nearby, called Ras Shamra.

300
00:21:31,851 --> 00:21:37,626
They soon realized that they had found
Ugarit, a city known until then only from

301
00:21:37,626 --> 00:21:41,596
mentions in the Amarna letters
and other foreign texts.

302
00:21:42,496 --> 00:21:47,531
Excavations have continued, on and
off, for most of a century since.

303
00:21:47,911 --> 00:21:53,539
What they have revealed is a city of
perhaps six to eight thousand people, with a

304
00:21:53,539 --> 00:21:58,958
great royal palace of around ninety
rooms, temples to the gods Baal and Dagan,

305
00:21:58,958 --> 00:22:04,517
wealthy private houses, and an adjoining
harbor that linked it to Cyprus, Egypt,

306
00:22:04,517 --> 00:22:06,184
Anatolia and the Aegean.

307
00:22:07,084 --> 00:22:10,280
But Ugarit's greatest
treasure was not gold.

308
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:11,847
It was words.

309
00:22:12,227 --> 00:22:17,750
Archaeologists have found thousands of clay
tablets there, written in more than half

310
00:22:17,750 --> 00:22:20,906
a dozen languages and
several different scripts.

311
00:22:21,287 --> 00:22:27,140
Among them was something extraordinary:
an alphabet of thirty letters, written in

312
00:22:27,140 --> 00:22:28,368
cuneiform wedges.

313
00:22:28,748 --> 00:22:34,230
One small tablet found there even lists
the letters in order, making it one of the

314
00:22:34,230 --> 00:22:37,105
oldest known alphabet
primers in the world.

315
00:22:38,005 --> 00:22:43,709
The texts written in that alphabet, in
the local Ugaritic language, include myths

316
00:22:43,709 --> 00:22:49,413
and epic poems about the god Baal, his
battles with the sea god Yam and with Mot,

317
00:22:49,413 --> 00:22:50,610
the god of death.

318
00:22:50,990 --> 00:22:56,608
Scholars of the Hebrew Bible have studied
them closely, because they share imagery

319
00:22:56,608 --> 00:23:00,582
and phrases with some of the
oldest passages of the Bible.

320
00:23:01,482 --> 00:23:03,554
And then there are the letters.

321
00:23:03,934 --> 00:23:08,880
Thousands of them, preserved in the
palace and in the houses of officials and

322
00:23:08,880 --> 00:23:09,522
merchants.

323
00:23:09,902 --> 00:23:15,459
Through them, we can follow Ugarit's final
years almost as if we were reading its

324
00:23:15,459 --> 00:23:15,802
mail.

325
00:23:16,702 --> 00:23:19,729
Ugarit was a vassal of the Hittites.

326
00:23:20,109 --> 00:23:26,141
Its kings owed loyalty, tribute, and military
service to the great king in Hattusa.

327
00:23:26,521 --> 00:23:31,343
And in its final years, the demands
from the Hittite court were relentless.

328
00:23:31,723 --> 00:23:32,895
Send grain.

329
00:23:33,275 --> 00:23:34,298
Send ships.

330
00:23:34,678 --> 00:23:35,802
Send troops.

331
00:23:36,702 --> 00:23:39,059
Then the letters start to change.

332
00:23:39,439 --> 00:23:44,549
One letter to the king of Ugarit from
the king of Cyprus warns him about enemy

333
00:23:44,549 --> 00:23:49,921
ships, telling him to fortify his cities,
bring his troops and chariots inside the

334
00:23:49,921 --> 00:23:52,804
walls, and wait for the
enemy with strength.

335
00:23:53,184 --> 00:23:58,390
Another letter from a Hittite official
speaks of a people called the Shikala, who

336
00:23:58,390 --> 00:24:03,661
live on ships, and asks for a man who had
been held captive by them to be sent for

337
00:24:03,661 --> 00:24:04,432
questioning.

338
00:24:05,332 --> 00:24:10,017
And then there is the letter we heard
at the very beginning of this story.

339
00:24:10,397 --> 00:24:15,789
Ammurapi, Ugarit's last king, writes to
the king of Cyprus, whom he addresses

340
00:24:15,789 --> 00:24:17,680
respectfully as his father.

341
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:19,964
The enemy's ships have come.

342
00:24:20,344 --> 00:24:22,452
His cities have been burned.

343
00:24:22,832 --> 00:24:26,176
His army is away in the
land of the Hittites.

344
00:24:26,556 --> 00:24:29,690
His fleet is away in the land of Lukka.

345
00:24:30,070 --> 00:24:33,410
Seven enemy ships have
done terrible damage.

346
00:24:34,310 --> 00:24:38,663
Here is where the story has often
been told with a powerful twist.

347
00:24:39,043 --> 00:24:44,525
For decades, it was said that some of these
final letters were found inside a kiln,

348
00:24:44,525 --> 00:24:49,809
where they had been placed to be baked
hard before being sent, and that the city

349
00:24:49,809 --> 00:24:52,583
fell before they could ever be dispatched.

350
00:24:52,963 --> 00:24:54,696
It is a haunting image.

351
00:24:55,076 --> 00:25:01,346
Unfortunately, more recent reexamination
of the excavation records suggests that it

352
00:25:01,346 --> 00:25:02,932
is probably not true.

353
00:25:03,312 --> 00:25:08,477
The tablets seem to have fallen from an
upper story when the building collapsed, and

354
00:25:08,477 --> 00:25:12,105
some were found in a different
place than the story claims.

355
00:25:13,005 --> 00:25:15,877
But the destruction itself is very real.

356
00:25:16,257 --> 00:25:22,052
Archaeologists found Ugarit burned, with
collapsed walls and a thick layer of ash.

357
00:25:22,432 --> 00:25:25,978
Arrowheads were scattered
through the streets of the city.

358
00:25:26,358 --> 00:25:31,669
Hoards of valuables had been buried and
never recovered, as if their owners hoped to

359
00:25:31,669 --> 00:25:33,819
come back for them, and never did.

360
00:25:34,719 --> 00:25:40,444
We can even date the end of Ugarit fairly
closely, thanks to another letter found in

361
00:25:40,444 --> 00:25:41,126
its ruins.

362
00:25:41,506 --> 00:25:47,439
It was sent by an Egyptian official named
Bay, a powerful chancellor in the Egyptian

363
00:25:47,439 --> 00:25:47,863
court.

364
00:25:48,243 --> 00:25:55,434
We know from Egyptian records that Bay
was executed in the fifth year of Pharaoh

365
00:25:55,434 --> 00:25:57,771
Siptah, around 1192 B.C.E.

366
00:25:58,151 --> 00:26:03,029
Since his letter reached Ugarit while he
was still alive, the city must have still

367
00:26:03,029 --> 00:26:04,159
been standing then.

368
00:26:04,539 --> 00:26:12,452
Most scholars now place its destruction
in the years shortly after, around 1190 to

369
00:26:12,452 --> 00:26:13,513
1185 B.C.E.

370
00:26:14,413 --> 00:26:17,314
After that, Ugarit was never rebuilt.

371
00:26:17,694 --> 00:26:20,612
The site remained empty for centuries.

372
00:26:20,992 --> 00:26:26,449
Its alphabet, its literature and its very
name disappeared from the world, until a

373
00:26:26,449 --> 00:26:30,841
farmer's plough struck stone more
than three thousand years later.

374
00:26:31,741 --> 00:26:36,716
Ugarit's overlord, the great Hittite
Empire, did not long outlive it.

375
00:26:37,096 --> 00:26:41,903
But what happened at the Hittite capital
is very different from what you might

376
00:26:41,903 --> 00:26:42,334
expect.

377
00:26:49,835 --> 00:26:56,086
High on the central Anatolian plateau,
about one hundred and fifty kilometers east

378
00:26:56,086 --> 00:27:02,032
of modern Ankara, lie the ruins of
Hattusa, the capital of the Hittite Empire.

379
00:27:02,412 --> 00:27:05,941
At its height, it was one of the
greatest cities in the world.

380
00:27:06,321 --> 00:27:11,716
Its walls ran for more than six
kilometers, studded with towers, pierced by

381
00:27:11,716 --> 00:27:16,751
monumental gates guarded by carved
lions, sphinxes, and a warrior god.

382
00:27:17,651 --> 00:27:24,157
Inside were temples, palaces, grain silos
and archives holding tens of thousands of

383
00:27:24,157 --> 00:27:25,176
clay tablets.

384
00:27:25,556 --> 00:27:28,660
The Hittites were
meticulous record keepers.

385
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:35,008
They wrote treaties, laws, rituals, prayers,
letters and royal annals in a variety

386
00:27:35,008 --> 00:27:40,759
of languages, chief among them Hittite
itself, the oldest written Indo-European

387
00:27:40,759 --> 00:27:41,996
language we know.

388
00:27:42,896 --> 00:27:46,330
The Hittites also thought
carefully about food.

389
00:27:46,710 --> 00:27:52,502
At Hattusa, archaeologists have found
enormous underground grain silos, one complex

390
00:27:52,502 --> 00:27:57,805
of which could have held several thousand
tons of grain, enough to feed many

391
00:27:57,805 --> 00:27:59,968
thousands of people for a year.

392
00:28:00,348 --> 00:28:06,571
These silos are a reminder that the Hittite
state understood how vulnerable it was

393
00:28:06,571 --> 00:28:07,785
to bad harvests.

394
00:28:08,165 --> 00:28:13,564
And yet, as we have seen, by the end of
the thirteenth century it was relying on

395
00:28:13,564 --> 00:28:14,779
grain from abroad.

396
00:28:15,679 --> 00:28:21,530
For a long time, scholars imagined the
end of Hattusa as a violent catastrophe.

397
00:28:21,910 --> 00:28:27,640
Enemies storming the walls, the great city
burning, the last Hittite king dying in

398
00:28:27,640 --> 00:28:28,479
its defense.

399
00:28:28,859 --> 00:28:31,026
Fire certainly struck the city.

400
00:28:31,406 --> 00:28:36,391
Parts of the citadel and some of the
temples show clear signs of burning.

401
00:28:37,291 --> 00:28:42,777
But the German archaeologists who have
excavated the site for more than a century

402
00:28:42,777 --> 00:28:44,877
have noticed something strange.

403
00:28:45,257 --> 00:28:50,938
When they dug into the palace and temple
quarters, they found remarkably little left

404
00:28:50,938 --> 00:28:51,411
behind.

405
00:28:51,791 --> 00:28:53,137
Few valuables.

406
00:28:53,517 --> 00:28:55,903
Few objects of everyday use.

407
00:28:56,283 --> 00:28:57,364
Few bodies.

408
00:28:57,744 --> 00:29:02,189
Buildings that look as if they had
been emptied before they were burned.

409
00:29:03,089 --> 00:29:09,206
Jürgen Seeher, who directed the excavations
for many years, argued that Hattusa was

410
00:29:09,206 --> 00:29:11,564
not sacked by a sudden invasion.

411
00:29:11,944 --> 00:29:17,422
Instead, he suggested, the royal court
abandoned it, packing up its treasures and

412
00:29:17,422 --> 00:29:22,899
its essential records and leaving for
somewhere else, perhaps somewhere easier to

413
00:29:22,899 --> 00:29:24,049
supply or defend.

414
00:29:24,429 --> 00:29:30,020
The fires may have come later, set by local
people or by squatters, after the great

415
00:29:30,020 --> 00:29:32,513
king and his court were already gone.

416
00:29:33,413 --> 00:29:39,346
That makes the end of the Hittite Empire
one of the most mysterious episodes of the

417
00:29:39,346 --> 00:29:40,490
entire collapse.

418
00:29:40,870 --> 00:29:45,276
We know the name of its last known
king, Suppiluliuma the Second.

419
00:29:45,656 --> 00:29:51,331
We know the empire was under severe stress
from famine, from rivals within the royal

420
00:29:51,331 --> 00:29:54,304
family, and from enemies
around its borders.

421
00:29:54,684 --> 00:29:59,291
But we do not know exactly what
happened to Suppiluliuma or his court.

422
00:29:59,671 --> 00:30:06,486
Sometime around 1180 B.C.E., the written
record of the great kings of Hatti simply

423
00:30:06,486 --> 00:30:06,985
stops.

424
00:30:07,885 --> 00:30:11,381
The Hittite name did
not disappear entirely.

425
00:30:11,761 --> 00:30:17,732
In the south, in cities like Carchemish
on the Euphrates, branches of the royal

426
00:30:17,732 --> 00:30:18,941
family survived.

427
00:30:19,321 --> 00:30:24,865
Over the following centuries, a group of
small kingdoms in southern Anatolia and

428
00:30:24,865 --> 00:30:29,370
northern Syria preserved Hittite
traditions, royal names and art.

429
00:30:29,750 --> 00:30:35,250
The Bible calls their peoples Hittites,
and Assyrian records called the region the

430
00:30:35,250 --> 00:30:36,189
land of Hatti.

431
00:30:36,569 --> 00:30:39,903
Scholars today call them
the Neo-Hittite states.

432
00:30:40,803 --> 00:30:46,366
But the great empire, the empire that had
matched Egypt at Kadesh, that had held a

433
00:30:46,366 --> 00:30:51,862
peace treaty with Ramesses, that had ruled
most of Anatolia and much of Syria for

434
00:30:51,862 --> 00:30:53,219
centuries, was gone.

435
00:30:54,119 --> 00:30:59,178
And its disappearance was so complete
that for nearly three thousand years, the

436
00:30:59,178 --> 00:31:01,163
Hittites were almost forgotten.

437
00:31:01,543 --> 00:31:03,488
Their language was lost.

438
00:31:03,868 --> 00:31:07,548
Their capital became a set
of ruins on a hillside.

439
00:31:07,928 --> 00:31:13,497
It was only in the late nineteenth and early
twentieth centuries that scholars began

440
00:31:13,497 --> 00:31:18,867
to rediscover them, and only in 1915 that
the Czech scholar Bedřich Hrozný showed

441
00:31:18,867 --> 00:31:21,121
that their language could be read.

442
00:31:22,021 --> 00:31:27,796
Meanwhile, far to the west, across the
Aegean Sea, another world of palaces was

443
00:31:27,796 --> 00:31:29,258
meeting its own end.

444
00:31:36,758 --> 00:31:42,564
On a hill in the northeastern Peloponnese,
overlooking the plain of Argos, stands

445
00:31:42,564 --> 00:31:44,213
the citadel of Mycenae.

446
00:31:44,593 --> 00:31:50,295
Its walls are built of stones so massive
that later Greeks believed they could only

447
00:31:50,295 --> 00:31:54,555
have been lifted by the Cyclopes,
the one-eyed giants of myth.

448
00:31:54,935 --> 00:31:58,844
We still call this style of
building Cyclopean masonry.

449
00:31:59,744 --> 00:32:05,960
You enter Mycenae through the Lion Gate,
built around 1250 B.C.E., with two great

450
00:32:05,960 --> 00:32:10,565
stone lions, or perhaps lionesses,
carved above the doorway.

451
00:32:10,945 --> 00:32:16,838
It is the oldest monumental sculpture in
Europe that still stands in its original

452
00:32:16,838 --> 00:32:17,274
place.

453
00:32:18,174 --> 00:32:25,079
In 1876, the German businessman and excavator
Heinrich Schliemann dug into a circle

454
00:32:25,079 --> 00:32:28,657
of royal shaft graves
just inside the gate.

455
00:32:29,037 --> 00:32:31,658
He found a staggering treasure of gold.

456
00:32:32,038 --> 00:32:37,049
Diadems, cups, jewelry, weapons,
and a set of gold funeral masks.

457
00:32:37,429 --> 00:32:42,813
Over one of them, he is said to have
announced that he had gazed upon the face of

458
00:32:42,813 --> 00:32:47,332
Agamemnon, the legendary king of
Mycenae who led the Greeks to Troy.

459
00:32:47,712 --> 00:32:52,953
In fact, the graves were centuries older
than any Trojan War could have been.

460
00:32:53,333 --> 00:32:54,829
But the name stuck.

461
00:32:55,209 --> 00:32:58,183
It is still called the Mask of Agamemnon.

462
00:32:59,083 --> 00:33:05,002
By the thirteenth century B.C.E., Mycenaean
Greece was a world of rival palace

463
00:33:05,002 --> 00:33:05,533
states.

464
00:33:05,913 --> 00:33:09,455
Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos,
Thebes, and others.

465
00:33:09,835 --> 00:33:14,103
Each palace was the center of
a highly organized economy.

466
00:33:14,483 --> 00:33:20,393
Scribes recorded, in great detail, the
land, livestock, workers, raw materials and

467
00:33:20,393 --> 00:33:23,420
finished goods that the palace controlled.

468
00:33:24,320 --> 00:33:27,355
They wrote in a script we call Linear B.

469
00:33:27,735 --> 00:33:30,297
For decades, nobody could read it.

470
00:33:30,677 --> 00:33:36,336
Then, in 1952, an English architect
named Michael Ventris, working with the

471
00:33:36,336 --> 00:33:41,542
philologist John Chadwick, showed
that it was an early form of Greek.

472
00:33:41,922 --> 00:33:47,082
Suddenly, the administrative records
of the Mycenaean palaces could speak.

473
00:33:47,982 --> 00:33:54,024
The most important collection of these
records came from the palace at Pylos in

474
00:33:54,024 --> 00:34:00,066
southwestern Greece, which the American
archaeologist Carl Blegen discovered in

475
00:34:00,066 --> 00:34:03,278
1939, on the very first day of excavation.

476
00:34:03,658 --> 00:34:07,249
He found more than a thousand
Linear B tablets there.

477
00:34:08,149 --> 00:34:10,155
Here is the strange thing.

478
00:34:10,535 --> 00:34:13,415
Linear B tablets were not meant to last.

479
00:34:13,795 --> 00:34:19,234
They were made of unbaked clay, like
temporary notes, and were regularly recycled.

480
00:34:19,614 --> 00:34:25,867
The only reason we have them at all is that
the palace of Pylos burned down, around

481
00:34:25,867 --> 00:34:29,333
1180 B.C.E., and the
fire baked the clay hard.

482
00:34:29,713 --> 00:34:34,747
The disaster that destroyed the palace
is what preserved its final records.

483
00:34:35,647 --> 00:34:38,712
And those final records are fascinating.

484
00:34:39,092 --> 00:34:43,178
Some tablets list rowers being
sent to a place called Pleuron.

485
00:34:43,558 --> 00:34:49,052
Others describe groups of men posted along
the coast, watchers, or guards of the

486
00:34:49,052 --> 00:34:52,485
coastline, as some scholars
have interpreted them.

487
00:34:52,865 --> 00:34:58,838
Some historians have read these texts as
emergency measures, a kingdom preparing for

488
00:34:58,838 --> 00:35:00,473
an attack from the sea.

489
00:35:00,853 --> 00:35:04,461
Others caution that these
could simply be routine.

490
00:35:04,841 --> 00:35:10,440
Either way, they offer a tantalizing
glimpse of the last weeks of a Bronze Age

491
00:35:10,440 --> 00:35:10,942
palace.

492
00:35:11,842 --> 00:35:13,673
Pylos was not alone.

493
00:35:14,053 --> 00:35:21,734
Across Greece, between roughly 1225 and
1175 B.C.E., the great palace centers were

494
00:35:21,734 --> 00:35:23,701
damaged or destroyed.

495
00:35:24,081 --> 00:35:26,424
Mycenae suffered destruction.

496
00:35:26,804 --> 00:35:30,038
Tiryns, with its massive
walls, was damaged.

497
00:35:30,418 --> 00:35:31,681
Thebes burned.

498
00:35:32,061 --> 00:35:35,624
Some sites were rebuilt for
a time on a smaller scale.

499
00:35:36,004 --> 00:35:38,526
Others were abandoned entirely.

500
00:35:39,426 --> 00:35:45,083
At Tiryns, excavators have found evidence
that has been interpreted as earthquake

501
00:35:45,083 --> 00:35:50,671
damage, including collapsed walls and,
in one case, skeletons that may have been

502
00:35:50,671 --> 00:35:52,766
crushed beneath falling stone.

503
00:35:53,146 --> 00:35:58,144
At Mycenae too, there are signs that
have been linked to seismic activity.

504
00:35:58,524 --> 00:36:02,048
We will return to the
question of earthquakes later.

505
00:36:02,948 --> 00:36:06,624
After the palaces fell,
life in Greece did not end.

506
00:36:07,004 --> 00:36:10,015
People continued to live in many places.

507
00:36:10,395 --> 00:36:16,105
In fact, for a few generations, some
communities even seem to have done reasonably

508
00:36:16,105 --> 00:36:16,453
well.

509
00:36:16,833 --> 00:36:22,734
A famous painted vessel from Mycenae, called
the Warrior Vase, shows a line of armed

510
00:36:22,734 --> 00:36:27,862
soldiers marching off, while a woman
raises her arm, perhaps in farewell.

511
00:36:28,242 --> 00:36:33,607
It is often dated to this period, just
after the destruction of the palaces.

512
00:36:34,507 --> 00:36:39,199
But the palace system, with its
scribes, its central storerooms and its

513
00:36:39,199 --> 00:36:41,776
long-distance trade, was gone for good.

514
00:36:42,156 --> 00:36:47,489
Within a few generations, population
levels across much of Greece appear to have

515
00:36:47,489 --> 00:36:48,889
dropped dramatically.

516
00:36:49,269 --> 00:36:51,584
Many settlements were abandoned.

517
00:36:51,964 --> 00:36:54,103
Monumental building stopped.

518
00:36:54,483 --> 00:36:58,653
And Linear B, the script of the
palaces, vanished completely.

519
00:36:59,553 --> 00:37:05,102
For roughly four centuries, the Greeks
lived in a world without writing, a period

520
00:37:05,102 --> 00:37:07,980
historians long called the Greek Dark Age.

521
00:37:08,360 --> 00:37:14,286
When writing finally returned, in the eighth
century B.C.E., it came in an entirely

522
00:37:14,286 --> 00:37:17,998
new form: an alphabet
borrowed from the Phoenicians.

523
00:37:18,378 --> 00:37:24,456
The memory of the palaces survived only
in myth, in stories of heroes, kings and a

524
00:37:24,456 --> 00:37:29,348
great war against Troy, eventually
set down in the poems of Homer.

525
00:37:30,248 --> 00:37:36,935
And Troy itself, on the coast of northwestern
Anatolia, was also destroyed in this

526
00:37:36,935 --> 00:37:42,969
period, around 1180 B.C.E., in the
layer archaeologists call Troy Seven A.

527
00:37:43,349 --> 00:37:48,895
Whether there was ever a real Trojan War,
and whether this destruction had anything

528
00:37:48,895 --> 00:37:53,372
to do with it, remains one of the
great debates of ancient history.

529
00:37:54,272 --> 00:37:57,013
In the east, the great empires were gone.

530
00:37:57,393 --> 00:38:00,577
In the west, the palaces
of Greece had fallen.

531
00:38:00,957 --> 00:38:03,515
One great power remained standing.

532
00:38:03,895 --> 00:38:09,770
But between Greece and Egypt lay an island
that would face the storm in its own way.

533
00:38:17,270 --> 00:38:22,769
Before we follow the storm to Egypt, we
need to stop on an island that sat right in

534
00:38:22,769 --> 00:38:23,365
its path.

535
00:38:23,745 --> 00:38:24,654
Cyprus.

536
00:38:25,034 --> 00:38:30,248
In the letters of the Late Bronze Age, it
was called Alashiya, and its kings wrote

537
00:38:30,248 --> 00:38:32,919
to Pharaoh as equals, calling him brother.

538
00:38:33,299 --> 00:38:35,237
The reason was simple.

539
00:38:35,617 --> 00:38:36,394
Copper.

540
00:38:37,294 --> 00:38:42,779
The copper mines in the foothills of the
Troodos Mountains were among the richest in

541
00:38:42,779 --> 00:38:43,954
the ancient world.

542
00:38:44,334 --> 00:38:49,523
In one of the Amarna letters, the king
of Alashiya sends five hundred units of

543
00:38:49,523 --> 00:38:55,110
copper to Egypt, and apologizes that it is
not more, explaining that the hand of the

544
00:38:55,110 --> 00:39:00,565
god Nergal, a god of plague and death, had
killed all the men of his land, so that

545
00:39:00,565 --> 00:39:03,226
there was no one left to produce copper.

546
00:39:03,606 --> 00:39:07,722
Even at the height of the age,
disaster was never far away.

547
00:39:08,622 --> 00:39:14,561
On the east coast of the island stood
Enkomi, a wealthy port city with streets laid

548
00:39:14,561 --> 00:39:20,071
out in a grid, fine houses built of cut
stone, and workshops where copper was

549
00:39:20,071 --> 00:39:21,288
smelted and cast.

550
00:39:21,668 --> 00:39:28,006
Further south, near modern Larnaca, lay
Hala Sultan Tekke, a large harbor town whose

551
00:39:28,006 --> 00:39:34,194
excavations have produced goods from across
the Mediterranean world, from Sardinia

552
00:39:34,194 --> 00:39:35,929
to Egypt to the Baltic.

553
00:39:36,829 --> 00:39:40,474
The people of Late Bronze
Age Cyprus also wrote.

554
00:39:40,854 --> 00:39:46,332
They used a script that we call Cypro-Minoan,
descended from the writing of Minoan

555
00:39:46,332 --> 00:39:46,733
Crete.

556
00:39:47,113 --> 00:39:49,536
And here is a remarkable fact.

557
00:39:49,916 --> 00:39:54,641
Even today, more than three thousand
years later, no one can read it.

558
00:39:55,021 --> 00:39:57,185
We can recognize the signs.

559
00:39:57,565 --> 00:40:02,009
We can see that it was used on
clay tablets, balls, and objects.

560
00:40:02,389 --> 00:40:05,536
But the language it
records is still a mystery.

561
00:40:06,436 --> 00:40:09,370
When the storm arrived,
Cyprus was hit hard.

562
00:40:09,750 --> 00:40:16,259
Around 1200 B.C.E., several sites show
signs of destruction or abandonment.

563
00:40:16,639 --> 00:40:21,548
At a place called Pyla-Kokkinokremos,
on a ridge overlooking the coast,

564
00:40:21,548 --> 00:40:27,148
archaeologists found a fortified settlement
that seems to have been occupied only

565
00:40:27,148 --> 00:40:31,711
briefly, perhaps for a single
generation, before it was abandoned.

566
00:40:32,091 --> 00:40:37,327
Hoards of metal and valuables were found
buried there, never recovered by their

567
00:40:37,327 --> 00:40:37,791
owners.

568
00:40:38,691 --> 00:40:44,245
At Maa-Palaeokastro, on a rocky headland
in the west of the island, a defended

569
00:40:44,245 --> 00:40:48,944
settlement with a wall across its
neck was built around this time.

570
00:40:49,324 --> 00:40:54,653
Some archaeologists have suggested that
the people who built these sites were

571
00:40:54,653 --> 00:40:59,983
newcomers from the Aegean, others that
they were Cypriots seeking safety in a

572
00:40:59,983 --> 00:41:00,952
dangerous age.

573
00:41:01,332 --> 00:41:06,426
As with so much of this period, the
evidence can be read in more than one way.

574
00:41:07,326 --> 00:41:13,519
But unlike the Hittites, unlike Ugarit, and
unlike the Mycenaean palaces, Cyprus did

575
00:41:13,519 --> 00:41:16,025
not simply collapse and disappear.

576
00:41:16,405 --> 00:41:19,067
Enkomi was damaged, but rebuilt.

577
00:41:19,447 --> 00:41:23,911
Kition, near Larnaca, was rebuilt
as well, with new temples.

578
00:41:24,291 --> 00:41:29,436
And it was in Enkomi, in the twelfth
century, that someone made one of the most

579
00:41:29,436 --> 00:41:31,716
striking bronze statues of the age.

580
00:41:32,096 --> 00:41:37,717
A warrior god, wearing a horned helmet,
holding a spear and shield, standing on a

581
00:41:37,717 --> 00:41:40,007
base shaped like an oxhide ingot.

582
00:41:40,387 --> 00:41:42,913
Scholars call him the Ingot God.

583
00:41:43,292 --> 00:41:47,701
The god of copper itself, standing
guard over the island's wealth.

584
00:41:48,601 --> 00:41:51,079
Cyprus seems to have adapted.

585
00:41:51,459 --> 00:41:53,940
Its copper industry continued.

586
00:41:54,320 --> 00:41:56,591
Its merchants found new partners.

587
00:41:56,971 --> 00:42:02,609
Over the following century, settlers who
spoke Greek arrived on the island, and by

588
00:42:02,609 --> 00:42:08,109
the Iron Age, Cyprus was a land of small
kingdoms where Greek was widely spoken,

589
00:42:08,109 --> 00:42:13,197
alongside other languages, some of
which we still cannot fully understand.

590
00:42:14,097 --> 00:42:19,791
Hala Sultan Tekke was eventually abandoned,
perhaps because its harbor was silting

591
00:42:19,791 --> 00:42:24,026
up, perhaps because the trade
that made it rich had dried up.

592
00:42:24,406 --> 00:42:30,399
But Cyprus as a whole survived the collapse
better than almost anywhere else in the

593
00:42:30,399 --> 00:42:30,904
region.

594
00:42:31,804 --> 00:42:32,563
Why?

595
00:42:32,943 --> 00:42:36,222
Historians are still
debating that question.

596
00:42:36,602 --> 00:42:43,076
One possibility is that Cyprus was less
dependent on a single, rigid palace system

597
00:42:43,076 --> 00:42:45,760
than places like Pylos or Hattusa.

598
00:42:46,140 --> 00:42:51,714
Its wealth was spread among several towns,
and its merchants were used to adapting

599
00:42:51,714 --> 00:42:53,073
to changing markets.

600
00:42:53,453 --> 00:42:58,698
In a world where the most centralized
states fell the hardest, a more flexible

601
00:42:58,698 --> 00:43:02,800
society may have had a better
chance of riding out the storm.

602
00:43:03,700 --> 00:43:08,571
But one great power was still to
face the full force of the crisis.

603
00:43:08,951 --> 00:43:14,678
A kingdom far older than any of the others,
which had seen empires rise and fall

604
00:43:14,678 --> 00:43:15,179
before.

605
00:43:15,559 --> 00:43:16,418
Egypt.

606
00:43:23,918 --> 00:43:29,309
On the west bank of the Nile, across
the river from modern Luxor, stands the

607
00:43:29,309 --> 00:43:34,842
memorial temple of Pharaoh Ramesses the
Third, at a place called Medinet Habu.

608
00:43:35,222 --> 00:43:38,944
It is one of the best preserved
temples in all of Egypt.

609
00:43:39,324 --> 00:43:45,187
Its walls are covered with colossal carved
scenes, many of them still bearing traces

610
00:43:45,187 --> 00:43:46,862
of their original paint.

611
00:43:47,762 --> 00:43:53,354
Ramesses the Third came to
the throne around 1184 B.C.E.

612
00:43:53,734 --> 00:43:58,621
He was not a descendant of Ramesses
the Second, though he took his name and

613
00:43:58,621 --> 00:44:02,139
deliberately modeled himself
on his great predecessor.

614
00:44:02,519 --> 00:44:06,776
He would become the last great
warrior pharaoh of the New Kingdom.

615
00:44:07,676 --> 00:44:13,702
And on the walls of Medinet Habu, he left
us the single most famous account of the

616
00:44:13,702 --> 00:44:14,584
Sea Peoples.

617
00:44:14,964 --> 00:44:21,653
In his eighth regnal year, which by the
most widely used chronology falls around

618
00:44:21,653 --> 00:44:26,168
1177 B.C.E., his scribes
recorded the following words.

619
00:44:27,068 --> 00:44:30,944
The foreign countries made a
conspiracy in their islands.

620
00:44:31,324 --> 00:44:35,523
All at once the lands were
removed and scattered in the fray.

621
00:44:35,903 --> 00:44:41,876
No land could stand before their arms,
from Hatti, Kode, Carchemish, Arzawa, and

622
00:44:41,876 --> 00:44:44,788
Alashiya on, being cut off at one time.

623
00:44:45,167 --> 00:44:48,427
A camp was set up in one place in Amurru.

624
00:44:48,807 --> 00:44:53,742
They desolated its people, and its land
was like that which has never come into

625
00:44:53,742 --> 00:44:54,117
being.

626
00:44:54,497 --> 00:44:59,420
They were coming forward toward Egypt,
while the flame was prepared before them.

627
00:45:00,320 --> 00:45:04,854
The inscription goes on to name
the members of this confederation.

628
00:45:05,234 --> 00:45:09,860
The Peleset, the Tjeker, the
Shekelesh, the Denyen and the Weshesh.

629
00:45:10,240 --> 00:45:11,815
Lands united.

630
00:45:12,715 --> 00:45:15,280
Then it describes two great battles.

631
00:45:15,660 --> 00:45:19,493
One on land, somewhere on
Egypt's border in the Levant.

632
00:45:19,873 --> 00:45:24,675
And one at sea, in the mouths of the
Nile, where the enemy fleet was trapped.

633
00:45:25,055 --> 00:45:30,744
Ramesses claims that he prepared the river
mouths like a strong wall, with warships,

634
00:45:30,744 --> 00:45:35,688
galleys and coasters, fully manned
from bow to stern with brave warriors.

635
00:45:36,588 --> 00:45:42,215
The carved scenes of this naval battle are
among the most famous images to survive

636
00:45:42,215 --> 00:45:43,793
from the ancient world.

637
00:45:44,173 --> 00:45:48,898
Egyptian ships, with lion-headed
prows, close in on the enemy.

638
00:45:49,278 --> 00:45:53,126
Egyptian archers fire from
the decks and from the shore.

639
00:45:53,506 --> 00:45:57,966
Enemy ships, with bird-headed
prows at both ends, are capsized.

640
00:45:58,346 --> 00:46:03,807
Their warriors, some wearing horned helmets,
others wearing distinctive headdresses

641
00:46:03,807 --> 00:46:09,203
that look like a crown of feathers or
reeds, fall into the water or are hauled out

642
00:46:09,203 --> 00:46:10,058
as prisoners.

643
00:46:10,958 --> 00:46:15,828
But perhaps the most revealing scene
is the one depicting the land battle.

644
00:46:16,208 --> 00:46:20,294
Among the warriors, there are ox
carts carrying women and children.

645
00:46:20,674 --> 00:46:22,592
These were not just armies.

646
00:46:22,972 --> 00:46:27,015
They were entire communities on
the move, looking for new land.

647
00:46:27,915 --> 00:46:31,041
According to the inscriptions, Egypt won.

648
00:46:31,421 --> 00:46:37,105
The invaders were defeated, captured,
branded and, in some cases, settled in

649
00:46:37,105 --> 00:46:39,498
Egyptian fortresses as soldiers.

650
00:46:39,878 --> 00:46:45,582
Ramesses the Third saved Egypt from the
fate that had befallen the Hittites and

651
00:46:45,582 --> 00:46:46,087
Ugarit.

652
00:46:46,987 --> 00:46:52,479
But the cost of victory was enormous, and
the decades that followed show a state

653
00:46:52,479 --> 00:46:53,852
under severe strain.

654
00:46:54,232 --> 00:46:59,455
In the twenty-ninth year of Ramesses the
Third, the workmen who built the royal

655
00:46:59,455 --> 00:47:04,943
tombs in the Valley of the Kings, living
in the village we now call Deir el-Medina,

656
00:47:04,943 --> 00:47:09,307
went on strike because their grain
rations had not been delivered.

657
00:47:09,687 --> 00:47:14,637
They laid down their tools and sat
behind the temples, refusing to work.

658
00:47:15,017 --> 00:47:19,235
It is the earliest well documented
labor strike in history.

659
00:47:20,135 --> 00:47:25,376
Then, toward the end of his reign, a
conspiracy formed within the royal harem.

660
00:47:25,756 --> 00:47:31,294
One of the king's secondary wives, Tiye,
plotted with courtiers and officials to

661
00:47:31,294 --> 00:47:35,448
murder Ramesses and place her
son, Pentawere, on the throne.

662
00:47:35,828 --> 00:47:39,877
Records of the trial that
followed survive on papyrus.

663
00:47:40,257 --> 00:47:45,676
Many conspirators were condemned to death,
and some were allowed to take their own

664
00:47:45,676 --> 00:47:46,072
lives.

665
00:47:46,972 --> 00:47:51,812
For a long time, it was unclear whether
the plot had actually succeeded.

666
00:47:52,192 --> 00:47:58,831
Then, in 2012, a team of scientists
published the results of CT scans of Ramesses

667
00:47:58,831 --> 00:48:00,306
the Third's mummy.

668
00:48:00,686 --> 00:48:05,897
They found a deep cut across his throat,
severe enough to have killed him almost

669
00:48:05,897 --> 00:48:10,392
instantly, which had been hidden
under the bandages by the embalmers.

670
00:48:10,772 --> 00:48:16,484
A later study also found that one of his
toes appears to have been cut off, perhaps

671
00:48:16,484 --> 00:48:17,791
in the same attack.

672
00:48:18,171 --> 00:48:22,568
The last great pharaoh of the New
Kingdom, it seems, was murdered.

673
00:48:23,468 --> 00:48:24,917
Egypt survived.

674
00:48:25,297 --> 00:48:27,264
But it was never the same.

675
00:48:27,644 --> 00:48:33,365
Over the following decades, under a series
of weaker kings who all bore the name

676
00:48:33,365 --> 00:48:37,442
Ramesses, Egypt lost control
of its empire in the Levant.

677
00:48:37,822 --> 00:48:40,953
Tomb robbing spread in
the Valley of the Kings.

678
00:48:41,333 --> 00:48:42,617
Prices rose.

679
00:48:42,997 --> 00:48:47,972
Royal power faded, and the priests
of Amun at Thebes grew more powerful.

680
00:48:48,352 --> 00:48:55,297
By around 1070 B.C.E., the New Kingdom
had come to an end, and Egypt was divided.

681
00:48:56,197 --> 00:48:58,249
And the defeated Sea Peoples?

682
00:48:58,629 --> 00:49:01,485
Some of them, at least, did not disappear.

683
00:49:01,865 --> 00:49:07,647
One group, the Peleset, settled on the
southern coast of Canaan, in cities like

684
00:49:07,647 --> 00:49:10,501
Ashkelon, Ashdod, Ekron, Gath and Gaza.

685
00:49:10,881 --> 00:49:13,089
We know them by another name.

686
00:49:13,469 --> 00:49:14,794
The Philistines.

687
00:49:22,294 --> 00:49:27,477
For more than a century, the Sea Peoples
have been the great villains of the Bronze

688
00:49:27,477 --> 00:49:28,289
Age collapse.

689
00:49:28,669 --> 00:49:34,087
In older history books, they appear as a
kind of barbarian horde, sweeping across

690
00:49:34,087 --> 00:49:38,970
the eastern Mediterranean, burning
everything in their path, and bringing

691
00:49:38,970 --> 00:49:40,709
civilization to its knees.

692
00:49:41,609 --> 00:49:45,255
The very name Sea Peoples
is a modern invention.

693
00:49:45,635 --> 00:49:51,417
It was popularized in the nineteenth
century by French Egyptologists, especially

694
00:49:51,417 --> 00:49:57,416
Gaston Maspero, who drew on phrases in the
Egyptian inscriptions about peoples from

695
00:49:57,416 --> 00:49:59,657
the sea and from their islands.

696
00:50:00,037 --> 00:50:05,393
The Egyptians themselves never used
it as a single label for one nation.

697
00:50:06,293 --> 00:50:08,046
So who were they really?

698
00:50:08,426 --> 00:50:11,750
The honest answer is that
we are still not certain.

699
00:50:12,130 --> 00:50:15,477
But we can say a few things
with some confidence.

700
00:50:16,377 --> 00:50:18,279
First, they were not one people.

701
00:50:18,659 --> 00:50:23,697
They were a shifting collection of groups
with different names, different dress, and

702
00:50:23,697 --> 00:50:25,316
probably different origins.

703
00:50:25,696 --> 00:50:31,056
Some had been known in the region for
generations as pirates, raiders or

704
00:50:31,056 --> 00:50:31,949
mercenaries.

705
00:50:32,329 --> 00:50:37,868
The Sherden, for example, served as
bodyguards to Ramesses the Second at Kadesh.

706
00:50:38,768 --> 00:50:43,063
Second, scholars have long tried
to link their names to places.

707
00:50:43,443 --> 00:50:48,303
The Sherden have been connected with
Sardinia, and the Shekelesh with Sicily.

708
00:50:48,683 --> 00:50:53,850
The Lukka almost certainly came
from Lycia in southwestern Anatolia.

709
00:50:54,230 --> 00:50:59,555
The Denyen have been linked to the Danaoi,
one of Homer's names for the Greeks, and

710
00:50:59,555 --> 00:51:01,864
the Ekwesh to the Achaeans, another.

711
00:51:02,244 --> 00:51:07,886
The Teresh have been connected, much more
speculatively, with the Tyrsenians, a name

712
00:51:07,886 --> 00:51:10,304
later Greeks used for the Etruscans.

713
00:51:10,684 --> 00:51:13,677
Some of these connections
are well supported.

714
00:51:14,057 --> 00:51:17,954
Others rest on little more
than a similarity of sound.

715
00:51:18,334 --> 00:51:23,760
And even where a name matches, it is often
unclear whether these peoples came from

716
00:51:23,760 --> 00:51:27,334
those places, or went there
later, after the collapse.

717
00:51:28,234 --> 00:51:33,697
Third, and most importantly, the archaeology
of the Philistines gives us a rare,

718
00:51:33,697 --> 00:51:36,906
direct glimpse of at
least one of these groups.

719
00:51:37,286 --> 00:51:43,760
In the early twelfth century B.C.E., at
sites like Ashdod, Ashkelon, Ekron and Gath,

720
00:51:43,760 --> 00:51:46,843
a new material culture suddenly appears.

721
00:51:47,223 --> 00:51:53,229
Locally made pottery painted in a style
that closely resembles Mycenaean pottery

722
00:51:53,229 --> 00:51:54,430
from the Aegean.

723
00:51:54,810 --> 00:51:57,016
Hearths of an Aegean type.

724
00:51:57,396 --> 00:52:00,361
Changes in diet, including more pork.

725
00:52:00,741 --> 00:52:03,521
Even new kinds of loom
weights for weaving.

726
00:52:04,421 --> 00:52:11,315
In 2019, a team of geneticists and
archaeologists published a study of ancient DNA

727
00:52:11,315 --> 00:52:14,174
from skeletons buried at Ashkelon.

728
00:52:14,554 --> 00:52:19,582
They found that people buried there
in the early Iron Age, right after the

729
00:52:19,582 --> 00:52:24,814
Philistines arrived, carried a distinct
genetic component that pointed toward

730
00:52:24,814 --> 00:52:29,910
southern Europe, a component that was
not present in the earlier Bronze Age

731
00:52:29,910 --> 00:52:30,657
population.

732
00:52:31,037 --> 00:52:36,601
Within a couple of centuries, that signal
had been largely diluted by intermarriage

733
00:52:36,601 --> 00:52:39,014
with the local Levantine population.

734
00:52:39,914 --> 00:52:45,332
In other words, at least some of the newcomers
really did come from the west, across

735
00:52:45,332 --> 00:52:45,848
the sea.

736
00:52:46,228 --> 00:52:49,525
But they did not wipe out the
people already living there.

737
00:52:49,905 --> 00:52:54,371
They settled among them, and their
descendants gradually blended in.

738
00:52:55,271 --> 00:52:58,888
This changes how we should
think about the Sea Peoples.

739
00:52:59,268 --> 00:53:04,842
Instead of a horde of destroyers who caused
the collapse, many historians now see

740
00:53:04,842 --> 00:53:07,801
them, at least in part,
as a symptom of it.

741
00:53:08,181 --> 00:53:13,916
People displaced by hunger, war and the
breakdown of their own societies, taking to

742
00:53:13,916 --> 00:53:16,887
the sea in search of
somewhere new to live.

743
00:53:17,267 --> 00:53:20,141
Some of them certainly raided and burned.

744
00:53:20,521 --> 00:53:23,891
Some fought as mercenaries
for whoever would pay.

745
00:53:24,271 --> 00:53:28,107
Some brought their families
in ox carts and settled down.

746
00:53:29,007 --> 00:53:32,863
And there is a deeper problem
with blaming them for everything.

747
00:53:33,243 --> 00:53:37,919
Many of the destroyed sites show no
clear sign of who destroyed them.

748
00:53:38,299 --> 00:53:41,816
At some, there is evidence
of fire but no weapons.

749
00:53:42,196 --> 00:53:44,853
At others, there are signs of earthquakes.

750
00:53:45,233 --> 00:53:50,830
And at some, like Hattusa, the evidence
points more toward abandonment than attack.

751
00:53:51,210 --> 00:53:53,295
The Sea Peoples were real.

752
00:53:53,675 --> 00:53:54,990
They were dangerous.

753
00:53:55,370 --> 00:53:58,412
But they cannot explain
everything on their own.

754
00:53:59,312 --> 00:54:04,974
To understand why so many great civilizations
fell at once, we have to look beyond

755
00:54:04,974 --> 00:54:09,462
the soldiers and ships, and into
the soil, the water and the sky.

756
00:54:16,962 --> 00:54:22,850
Over the past two decades, a new kind of
evidence has transformed the debate about

757
00:54:22,850 --> 00:54:24,717
the end of the Bronze Age.

758
00:54:25,097 --> 00:54:28,290
It does not come from
palaces or inscriptions.

759
00:54:28,670 --> 00:54:33,261
It comes from mud, from pollen, and
from the rings of ancient trees.

760
00:54:34,161 --> 00:54:40,187
In 2013, a team of researchers led by
Dafna Langgut published the results of a

761
00:54:40,187 --> 00:54:44,590
sediment core drilled from
the bed of the Sea of Galilee.

762
00:54:44,970 --> 00:54:50,551
Layer by layer, the mud preserved tiny
grains of pollen, a record of the plants

763
00:54:50,551 --> 00:54:53,800
growing in the region
over thousands of years.

764
00:54:54,180 --> 00:54:59,678
Toward the end of the Late Bronze Age, the
pollen of trees like oak, pine and olive

765
00:54:59,678 --> 00:55:00,738
dropped sharply.

766
00:55:01,118 --> 00:55:07,064
This is a sign of a much drier climate,
a long stretch of drought that the

767
00:55:07,064 --> 00:55:11,483
researchers placed roughly
between 1250 and 1100 B.C.E.

768
00:55:12,383 --> 00:55:18,803
That same year, a team led by David Kaniewski
published a study of sediments from a

769
00:55:18,803 --> 00:55:24,836
salt lake near Larnaca, on Cyprus, close
to the Bronze Age harbor city of Hala

770
00:55:24,836 --> 00:55:25,841
Sultan Tekke.

771
00:55:26,221 --> 00:55:31,580
It also pointed to a shift to drier
conditions at about this time, a period of

772
00:55:31,580 --> 00:55:36,046
drought that seems to have lasted
for as long as three centuries.

773
00:55:36,946 --> 00:55:43,245
Then, in 2023, a study led by Sturt Manning,
published in the journal Nature, looked

774
00:55:43,245 --> 00:55:49,393
at the growth rings of ancient juniper
wood from Gordion, in central Anatolia, the

775
00:55:49,393 --> 00:55:51,718
heart of the old Hittite lands.

776
00:55:52,098 --> 00:55:56,825
Tree rings grow thicker in wet
years and thinner in dry ones.

777
00:55:57,205 --> 00:56:04,037
The researchers found a severe drought
in this region, and in particular three

778
00:56:04,037 --> 00:56:09,993
consecutive extremely dry years,
dated to around 1198 to 1196 B.C.E.

779
00:56:10,893 --> 00:56:16,850
Three years in a row of failed harvests,
in a region where the great Hittite state

780
00:56:16,850 --> 00:56:19,393
already depended on imported grain.

781
00:56:19,773 --> 00:56:25,700
That is exactly when we see the desperate
letters about grain shipments, a matter of

782
00:56:25,700 --> 00:56:26,688
life or death.

783
00:56:27,068 --> 00:56:31,227
It fits the timeline of the
Hittite collapse disturbingly well.

784
00:56:32,127 --> 00:56:35,711
But drought was not the
only natural force at work.

785
00:56:36,091 --> 00:56:42,509
In 2000, the geophysicist Amos Nur and
the archaeologist Eric Cline proposed what

786
00:56:42,509 --> 00:56:45,044
they called an earthquake storm.

787
00:56:45,424 --> 00:56:50,489
The eastern Mediterranean is one of the
most seismically active regions in the

788
00:56:50,489 --> 00:56:50,879
world.

789
00:56:51,259 --> 00:56:57,178
Nur and Cline argued that over a period of
perhaps fifty years, a sequence of large

790
00:56:57,178 --> 00:57:03,169
earthquakes could have struck many of the
major Bronze Age sites, including Mycenae,

791
00:57:03,169 --> 00:57:05,451
Tiryns, Troy, Ugarit and others.

792
00:57:06,351 --> 00:57:11,581
Earthquakes alone probably would not
have brought down entire civilizations.

793
00:57:11,961 --> 00:57:17,573
Societies have survived earthquakes throughout
history, rebuilding again and again.

794
00:57:17,953 --> 00:57:22,769
But earthquakes striking already weakened
states, at a time when their resources

795
00:57:22,769 --> 00:57:25,718
were stretched thin, could
have been devastating.

796
00:57:26,618 --> 00:57:28,935
Then there are the human factors.

797
00:57:29,315 --> 00:57:33,978
Some scholars have suggested that
internal rebellion played a part.

798
00:57:34,358 --> 00:57:40,064
When harvests fail and palaces keep demanding
taxes and labor, ordinary people may

799
00:57:40,064 --> 00:57:42,361
have turned against their rulers.

800
00:57:42,741 --> 00:57:48,097
Some of the destroyed palaces could have
been burned not by foreign invaders, but by

801
00:57:48,097 --> 00:57:49,309
their own subjects.

802
00:57:50,209 --> 00:57:52,685
And there is the network itself.

803
00:57:53,065 --> 00:57:59,128
Remember the Uluburun ship, with its copper
from Cyprus and its tin from the distant

804
00:57:59,128 --> 00:57:59,489
east.

805
00:57:59,869 --> 00:58:04,334
Every palace depended on these
long, fragile chains of trade.

806
00:58:04,714 --> 00:58:09,973
If drought hit one region, if earthquakes
struck another, if pirates made the sea

807
00:58:09,973 --> 00:58:15,232
lanes too dangerous to sail, if a key
trading city like Ugarit was destroyed, the

808
00:58:15,232 --> 00:58:18,543
effects would have spread
through the whole system.

809
00:58:19,443 --> 00:58:24,983
This is the explanation that the historian
Eric Cline, whose book on the subject

810
00:58:24,983 --> 00:58:27,753
gave this date its fame, has championed.

811
00:58:28,133 --> 00:58:29,846
A systems collapse.

812
00:58:30,226 --> 00:58:32,985
Not a single cause, but many at once.

813
00:58:33,365 --> 00:58:35,898
Climate change, drought and famine.

814
00:58:36,278 --> 00:58:37,299
Earthquakes.

815
00:58:37,679 --> 00:58:39,951
Invasion and migration.

816
00:58:40,331 --> 00:58:42,136
Internal unrest.

817
00:58:42,516 --> 00:58:45,345
The breakdown of international trade.

818
00:58:45,725 --> 00:58:49,264
Each of these, on its own,
might have been survivable.

819
00:58:49,644 --> 00:58:54,810
Together, they reinforced one another,
in a cascade of failures that no single

820
00:58:54,810 --> 00:58:56,069
kingdom could stop.

821
00:58:56,969 --> 00:59:03,109
Complex societies, as the historian Joseph
Tainter has argued, can become vulnerable

822
00:59:03,109 --> 00:59:05,887
precisely because of their complexity.

823
00:59:06,267 --> 00:59:11,722
The more specialized and interconnected
they are, the more their survival depends on

824
00:59:11,722 --> 00:59:13,476
everything working at once.

825
00:59:13,856 --> 00:59:19,664
The Late Bronze Age palaces were masters
of complexity: central administrations,

826
00:59:19,664 --> 00:59:25,618
scribes, specialized workshops, and trade
routes spanning thousands of kilometers.

827
00:59:25,998 --> 00:59:29,410
When the shocks came, they
had a very long way to fall.

828
00:59:30,310 --> 00:59:32,982
It is important to be careful here.

829
00:59:33,362 --> 00:59:37,705
Not every region collapsed at
the same time or in the same way.

830
00:59:38,085 --> 00:59:42,355
Some places, like Cyprus,
recovered relatively quickly.

831
00:59:42,735 --> 00:59:45,144
Assyria survived, though weakened.

832
00:59:45,524 --> 00:59:47,044
Egypt held on.

833
00:59:47,424 --> 00:59:52,914
The collapse was not one event on one
day, but a process unfolding over several

834
00:59:52,914 --> 00:59:57,501
decades, with different causes
mattering more in different places.

835
00:59:58,401 --> 01:00:01,473
But the overall pattern is unmistakable.

836
01:00:01,853 --> 01:00:06,974
In a few generations, the world of the
great kings, of the Amarna letters, of

837
01:00:06,974 --> 01:00:09,435
brother writing to brother, was gone.

838
01:00:16,935 --> 01:00:21,422
Historians often describe
what came next as a dark age.

839
01:00:21,802 --> 01:00:24,160
And in many places, it was.

840
01:00:24,539 --> 01:00:26,044
Populations fell.

841
01:00:26,424 --> 01:00:28,794
Cities shrank or disappeared.

842
01:00:29,174 --> 01:00:31,490
Long-distance trade dwindled.

843
01:00:31,870 --> 01:00:34,028
Writing vanished from Greece.

844
01:00:34,408 --> 01:00:38,118
Monumental art and
architecture largely stopped.

845
01:00:39,018 --> 01:00:41,799
But collapse is never only an ending.

846
01:00:42,179 --> 01:00:47,441
Out of the ruins of the Bronze Age, a new
world slowly emerged, and it would turn

847
01:00:47,441 --> 01:00:50,040
out to be the world that shaped our own.

848
01:00:50,940 --> 01:00:53,849
The first great change was a new metal.

849
01:00:54,229 --> 01:00:59,371
Iron had been known and worked for centuries,
but it had been a rare and precious

850
01:00:59,371 --> 01:00:59,942
material.

851
01:01:00,322 --> 01:01:05,990
As the trade in tin broke down, and as
smiths learned better ways of working iron,

852
01:01:05,990 --> 01:01:09,999
it gradually became the common
metal of tools and weapons.

853
01:01:10,379 --> 01:01:14,481
Unlike copper and tin, iron
ore is found almost everywhere.

854
01:01:14,861 --> 01:01:20,104
You did not need a palace, a fleet or a
network of foreign kings to obtain it.

855
01:01:20,484 --> 01:01:23,454
In a sense, iron democratized metal.

856
01:01:24,354 --> 01:01:28,180
The second great change came
from the coast of the Levant.

857
01:01:28,560 --> 01:01:33,817
Some of the old Canaanite cities, like
Byblos, Sidon and Tyre, survived the

858
01:01:33,817 --> 01:01:36,550
collapse, or recovered quickly from it.

859
01:01:36,930 --> 01:01:42,162
Their people, whom the Greeks would later
call the Phoenicians, became the great

860
01:01:42,162 --> 01:01:44,385
seafaring traders of the Iron Age.

861
01:01:44,765 --> 01:01:50,298
Their ships sailed farther than any Bronze
Age merchants had ever gone, all the way

862
01:01:50,298 --> 01:01:55,098
across the Mediterranean, and eventually
beyond the Strait of Gibraltar.

863
01:01:55,478 --> 01:02:00,866
They founded colonies, including, according
to tradition, the city of Carthage.

864
01:02:01,766 --> 01:02:05,360
And they spread something that
would change the world forever.

865
01:02:05,740 --> 01:02:06,931
The alphabet.

866
01:02:07,310 --> 01:02:12,696
The Phoenician alphabet, a simple set of
twenty-two letters, could be learned in

867
01:02:12,696 --> 01:02:14,244
days rather than years.

868
01:02:14,624 --> 01:02:18,558
Writing was no longer the
secret skill of palace scribes.

869
01:02:18,938 --> 01:02:24,442
In the eighth century B.C.E., the Greeks
adopted it, adding letters for vowels.

870
01:02:24,822 --> 01:02:29,999
From the Greek alphabet came the Etruscan,
then the Latin alphabet, the one you are

871
01:02:29,999 --> 01:02:32,370
reading on your screen at this moment.

872
01:02:33,270 --> 01:02:38,785
In the southern Levant, in the space left
by the retreat of Egypt and the fall of

873
01:02:38,785 --> 01:02:43,142
the Canaanite city-states, new
peoples and new kingdoms emerged.

874
01:02:43,522 --> 01:02:45,942
The Philistines on the coast.

875
01:02:46,322 --> 01:02:49,851
The kingdoms of Israel and
Judah in the highlands.

876
01:02:50,231 --> 01:02:52,932
Aram, Ammon, Moab and Edom.

877
01:02:53,312 --> 01:02:58,942
Many of the stories of the Hebrew Bible
are set in this new Iron Age world.

878
01:02:59,842 --> 01:03:06,127
In Mesopotamia, Assyria survived its own
crises and, a few centuries later, rose to

879
01:03:06,127 --> 01:03:12,336
become a new kind of empire, larger and
more ruthless than anything the Bronze Age

880
01:03:12,336 --> 01:03:13,017
had seen.

881
01:03:13,917 --> 01:03:19,916
And in Greece, after centuries of silence,
a new civilization would rise, one of

882
01:03:19,916 --> 01:03:26,064
independent city-states, of the Olympic
games, of philosophy, democracy and drama.

883
01:03:26,444 --> 01:03:29,856
But they never forgot the
age that had come before.

884
01:03:30,236 --> 01:03:35,635
Their poets sang of a lost age of heroes,
of mighty kings who lived in golden

885
01:03:35,635 --> 01:03:38,650
Mycenae, and of a great
war fought at Troy.

886
01:03:39,031 --> 01:03:44,802
The Iliad and the Odyssey, the founding
works of Western literature, are in part a

887
01:03:44,802 --> 01:03:48,251
distant memory of the
Bronze Age world that fell.

888
01:03:49,151 --> 01:03:54,133
So what really killed the great
civilizations of the Late Bronze Age?

889
01:03:54,513 --> 01:03:56,423
Not a single invasion.

890
01:03:56,803 --> 01:03:59,038
Not a single catastrophe.

891
01:03:59,418 --> 01:04:02,438
The evidence points to
a deadly combination.

892
01:04:02,818 --> 01:04:05,033
Years of drought and famine.

893
01:04:05,413 --> 01:04:06,434
Earthquakes.

894
01:04:06,814 --> 01:04:08,674
Rebellion from within.

895
01:04:09,054 --> 01:04:11,690
Peoples uprooted and on the move.

896
01:04:12,070 --> 01:04:17,862
And a tightly connected international system
that turned each local disaster into a

897
01:04:17,862 --> 01:04:18,630
shared one.

898
01:04:19,530 --> 01:04:23,411
Perhaps that is why this
story feels so relevant today.

899
01:04:23,791 --> 01:04:30,098
The world of 1177 B.C.E. was one of
interconnected economies, of supply chains

900
01:04:30,098 --> 01:04:36,566
stretching across continents, of rulers
who believed their prosperity would last

901
01:04:36,566 --> 01:04:37,213
forever.

902
01:04:37,593 --> 01:04:42,423
It was a world that seemed too big,
too rich and too connected to fail.

903
01:04:43,323 --> 01:04:48,826
And when it did fail, the last king of
Ugarit wrote to his father in Cyprus, asking

904
01:04:48,826 --> 01:04:50,881
for help that would never come.

905
01:04:51,781 --> 01:04:54,757
Thank you for watching
Ancient World Files.

906
01:04:55,137 --> 01:05:00,581
If you enjoyed this journey into the end
of the Bronze Age, subscribe for more

907
01:05:00,581 --> 01:05:05,816
evidence-based deep dives into the lost
civilizations of the ancient world.

